{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And if three these.</b> They are her food, her clothing, and her conjugal rights, and therefore the verse is precise in saying <b>these</b> and not those preceding them, which are designation and so forth. And it says <b>and she shall go out free</b> — meaning, their rights over her are voided, for they can no longer hold her until the end of six years or other signs, but rather either he must fulfill these three things or he must send her out with a bill of divorce, and therefore it concludes by saying <b>without money</b> — to say: it is money that is not required, but a bill of divorce is required, for she is his betrothed. And our Sages of blessed memory (Mekhilta) said: these three are — designation to himself, to his son, and reduction of money; and she goes out free through the advent of signs, and these are among the laws stated at Mount Sinai and are attached to them." "<b>Now it appears</b> to interpret this passage by way of remez according to their words, for they said (Sotah 42b) that an unspecified אִישׁ (\"man\") means none other than HaKadosh Barukh Hu, as it is written (Exodus 15:3) ״ה׳ אִישׁ מִלְחָמָה״. And they further said (Shir HaShirim Rabbah ch. 3) that HaShem did not cease loving Knesset Yisrael until He called her His daughter, and we find that Scripture called the matter of exile by the name מְכִירָה (\"selling\"), as it is written (I Samuel 12:9) ״וַיִּמְכֹּר אוֹתָם בְּיַד״ and so forth. And behold, according to what preceded — that Scripture commanded concerning one who acquires a Hebrew slave that he may not work him except for six years — therefore Scripture pleads on behalf of the people of Yisrael: why has her law changed from the law of slaves who go out in the sixth year? And that is its saying <b>and if a man sell his daughter to be a maid servant</b> — why should she be diminished from the law of slaves, which You commanded for six years and so forth? And that is its saying <b>she shall not go out as the slaves go out</b> in the seventh year. And why was she sold in this bitter exile — this one, to us, is one thousand seven hundred and seventy-two — and we have not been saved? And grant us the law of the Hebrew slave as recorded in writing." "And Scripture returned and pressed the charge: <b>if evil</b> and so forth — meaning, if she still stands at the level of wickedness for which she was sold, as it is written (Isaiah 50:1) ״הֵן בַּעֲוֹנוֹתֵיכֶם נִמְכַּרְתֶּם״ and so forth. And its saying <b>in the eyes of her master, who</b> and so forth — meaning, for the precious daughter exists also at the level of a bride, as stated in the holy of holies, the Song of Songs (Song of Songs 4:8), and that is its saying <b>her master, who designated her</b> — for her He designated to be a bride. And it is written with an alef: <b>who did not designate her</b> — meaning, now He did not designate her on account of her ugly deeds. And Scripture pressed the charge concerning this: <b>and he shall redeem her</b> — meaning, for the aspect of evil cannot be recognized in her while she is still sold and her masters are alien lords, for they prevent her from doing good and compel her to do evil in the eyes of her masters, and her being evil in exile is no answer until there is redemption — then it can be recognized in her whether she is evil or not. And from now on it is not just that He should sell her to a foreign people because she is evil in His eyes all the time that He deals treacherously with her — meaning, all the time of her sale — but rather on account of what she did in the days of her tranquility, when there was no foreign ruler's hand upon her." "And its saying <b>and if to his son</b> — He pressed the charge and again pressed the charge, for we find that HaKadosh Barukh Hu called Yisrael \"sons\" in a proclamation before the eyes of all the nations, and they knew that Yisrael is a nation designated by the name \"son of HaShem.\" And now, if this nation is still designated for the aspect of \"His son,\" it would have been fitting at the very least that she not be inferior to the nations, as our eyes see the exalted state of the nations and their prosperity — which is not so for Yisrael — and it would be fitting that she not be inferior to them. And the humiliation that Yisrael should not have a rank greater than theirs would be sufficient. And that is its saying <b>according to the law of daughters</b> — for they too are called \"daughters,\" but they could not qualify for this rank to be called the daughter of HaKadosh Barukh Hu. And its saying <b>if another</b> — know that they said, of blessed memory (Zohar II 158a), that at the time of the Destruction all the vessels of influence pour forth outside their place, to the other nations, and not to Yisrael, for the channels are broken and drain into an unholy place. And that is its saying <b>if he takes another</b> — to give the measure of His goodness, as the eyes of all Yisrael see the abundance of good in the hand of those who are not of the children of Yisrael. Scripture further pressed the charge: even if there is still anger against her, so that she is not at the level of the good and tranquility of the daughters of the world, and HaShem gives the measure of His goodness to another nation — at the very least her food, her clothing, and her conjugal rights He should not diminish. Her food — these are her provisions, as Rabbi Yoshiya said (Mekhilta); her clothing — as its meaning; and her conjugal rights — this is that He should answer her in her distress. And these three things are necessities, and how could she live without them?" "And it further says <b>and if three these</b> — meaning, her food, her clothing, and her conjugal rights, He does not provide for her, and she is inferior to them. <b>And she shall go out free</b> — meaning, even though there is not in their hand neither Torah nor commandments, and they do not even yearn for the matter of redemption — and that is its saying <b>without money</b>, for her sufferings suffice for her redemption." "<b>Or</b> the passage alludes in this manner: <b>וְכִי יִמְכֹּר אִישׁ</b> — this is the Holy One, blessed be He; <b>אֶת בִּתּוֹ</b> — the holy nation; <b>לְאָמָה</b> — meaning, to be a maidservant, for already the exiles have passed over Israel: the exile of Egypt, the exile of Babylon, etc., and in all of them Israel was not enslaved except to one nation, and this does not constitute calling the name of servitude upon Israel. But in the present exile in which we now are, Israel is enslaved to all seventy nations; in every place where a Hebrew is found he is despised and lowly, and each one plunders and goes back gathering scrap after scrap, and this matter tests and marks them with a great trial and a glaring sign. And this is what it says <b>לְאָמָה</b>, for our nation in this exile acquired the name of a maidservant, and from now on even when our redemption comes and the ransom of our soul, the nation has already acquired the name of servitude. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, made a promise that the redemption of this nation will not be in the manner in which YHWH brought us out of Egypt from the house of slaves, but with wondrous miracles publicized throughout the entire world. And this is what it says <b>לֹא תֵצֵא כְּצֵאת הָעֲבָדִים</b> — those alluded to in the Torah (Exodus 20:2): ״מִבֵּית עֲבָדִים״ etc., for then YHWH did not perform His wonders except upon one nation, but then it will be in all the world, and kings shall be your nursing fathers and their princesses etc., and all kings shall bow down, as written in the book of Isaiah (Isaiah 49:23)." "And what it says <b>אִם רָעָה</b> etc. is explained according to the statement of the Zohar, that the redemption of Israel changes: if it comes about by means of their good deeds, or if it comes about on account of the complete end (keitz). For if it comes about by means of Israel's preparation, the redeemer will come as the angel of YHWH in a pillar of fire standing on the earth whose head reaches the heavens; but if on account of the keitz and Israel are not worthy of it, concerning this it is said (Zechariah 9:9): ״עָנִי וְרוֹכֵב עַל חֲמוֹר״. And this is what it says regarding the coming of the redeemer on account of the keitz even though Israel are not worthy of it — <b>אִם רָעָה</b> etc., meaning even if she is evil in the eyes of her master, when the keitz that He has designated for her appointed time arrives, <b>וְלֹא</b> — with an aleph, it further intends that she is not worthy of His designations for she is evil; even so <b>וְהֶפְדָּהּ</b>, meaning, a ransom will be granted to her in any case, but the matter will not be as it would be if she were not evil at the time of the redemption. And it says <b>לְעַם נָכְרִי</b> etc., meaning, that the sale to a foreign nation will not be forever even though he betrays her, as the verse says (Job 28:3): ״קֵץ שָׂם לַחֹשֶׁךְ״." "And corresponding to the coming of the redemption by virtue of their good deeds and their worthiness, it says <b>וְאִם לִבְנוֹ יִיעָדֶנָּה</b>, after the saying of the Sages (Bava Batra 10a) that when Israel do the will of the Place they are called sons. The intention is: if the designations that YHWH designates for her at the time of redemption will be on account of their being in the degree of sons, then <b>כְּמִשְׁפַּט הַבָּנוֹת</b>. This matter declares the surpassing delight of Israel at the coming of the redeemer — meaning, daughters are the souls that delight in the Garden of Eden, who are called the daughters of Jerusalem, and the ordinance that the bundle of myrrh of my beloved has decreed for them — לְהִגָּלוֹת לָהֶם אוֹרוֹ יִתְבָּרַךְ — to reveal to them His light, blessed be He, and this is their sustenance and delight and the ultimate object of their longing. HaShem said that according to this ordinance it shall be done for them at the coming of the redeemer while they are still in this world — HaShem will reveal the light of His Shekhinah upon all the righteous, and they will all say ״זֶה אֵלִי״ etc. in constancy, according to the ordinance of the daughters." "And what it says <b>אִם אַחֶרֶת</b> etc. — the verse comes to stipulate: when did I distinguish for you between a time when she is evil and a time when she is in the degree of a son? Only in a time when he takes another, in the manner we explained above — <b>שְׁאֵרָהּ כְסוּתָהּ וְעֹנָתָהּ</b> of the Israelite daughter <b>לֹא יִגְרָע</b> — in this is what I distinguished. But <b>אִם שְׁלָשׁ אֵלֶּה</b> etc. — <b>וְיָצְאָה חִנָּם</b>, meaning, even if they are not righteous enough to long for the day of redemption, they will depart even before the time of the exile, for they go out in the merit of the sufferings they accepted through the lack of the three things, and they are equivalent to the degree of a son."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {1 "The date זה לנו אלף ותרע״ב = 1772 (532 from the destruction of the Temple, counted in thousands). The reading ותרע״ב is preserved literally as given in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:26.561111619Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}